  "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2032}+4y} & =L{2+t} \\\\ L{{y}^{\u2032}}+4L{y} & =2L{1}+L{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{\u2032}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=3:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{\u2032}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-3\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},\\,s>0,\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2032}}+4L{y} & =2L{1}+L{t} \\\\ (sY(s)-3)+4Y(s) & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =3+\\frac{2}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+2s+1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+2s+1}{{s}^{2}(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
